\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Pullback of polynomial functions}
\date{2026-10-23}

\p{Let #{V} be finite, #{J\subseteq V}, #{X=\mathbb R^V} and
#{Y=\mathbb R^J}. Let #{\pi:X\to Y} restrict a configuration to #{J}.
Write #{\mathcal P(X)} and #{\mathcal P(Y)} for the algebras of real
polynomial functions generated by the coordinate evaluation functions,
with pointwise operations and the identity involution. Then
##{\begin{aligned}
\pi^*:\mathcal P(Y)&\longrightarrow\mathcal P(X),&
P&\longmapsto P\circ\pi
\end{aligned}}
is an injective unital real algebra map preserving these involutions.
Every supplied [real algebraic state](fdip-0024) on #{\mathcal P(X)}
therefore pulls back to one on #{\mathcal P(Y)}.}
\proof{
\p{Each coordinate function #{y\mapsto y_j}, #{j\in J}, pulls back
to #{x\mapsto x_j}. Composition preserves sums, products, real scalar
multiples and constants by pointwise evaluation. Consequently a
polynomial function pulls back to a polynomial function and #{\pi^*}
is a unital real algebra map. Both involutions are the identity, so it
preserves star. Define #{s:Y\to X} by filling coordinates outside #{J}
with zero. Then #{\pi\circ s=\mathrm{id}_Y}. If #{P\circ\pi=Q\circ\pi},
composition with #{s} gives #{P=Q}, proving injectivity. The
[state pullback proposition](fdip-0027) now applies to a supplied state.
These arguments also cover #{J=\varnothing} and #{V=\varnothing}; their
real coordinate spaces are singletons, not empty sets.}
}
\p{Here polynomial functions are actual functions, rather than formal
polynomial expressions. The proof uses coordinate composition and the
zero-fill section, not a finiteness assertion about the real set #{X}.
The algebra and positivity conventions are
\citet{Section 22.2, Tag 09JF}{stacks2017stacks} and
\citet{Definition 3.9, p. 11}{waldmann2004states}.
The finite-map construction gives the same contravariant direction,
but its finite indicator expansion is not used here.}

\p{For the [Gaussian expectation](fdip-0006), measure and polynomial
[integrability](fdip-002W) first establishes that the
[Gaussian polynomial state](fdip-002X) on #{\mathcal P(X)} exists. Once supplied, it restricts by the algebraic result above.
Identifying its retained law requires the separate
[Gaussian marginal calculation](fdip-000E). This proposition neither
extends that analytic calculation to new degenerate cases nor asserts
that all integrable functions form an algebra. Polynomial functions are
also distinct from the [algebra of field endomorphisms](fdip-0011).}
